  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 156 PM EDT Thu Sep 17 2026\n\nActive weather to continue through at least Monday as our area \nremains on the dreaded N/NE fringes of large mid/upper level ridge. \nHeavy rain this morning is finally ending with a brief break \nexpected late this afternoon. However, additional storms are \npossible later this evening (esp south of US-24) as models indicate \nanother weak shortwave possibly developing along the sharp \nbaroclinic zone currently in place. Confidence is low however as \nthere is relatively high spread among various models (including the \nCAM's) regarding timing, location, and intensity of any convection \nthat does manage to develop due to incredibly weak/subtle synoptic \nforcing overnight. Tend to favor the HRRR here which shows a more \nmuted convective response focused just south of our area where \nremnant outflow boundary likely sets up. The idea that the bulk of \nthe activity will be focused just to our south has reasonable \nagreement from other CAM's but the HRRR is on the far end of the \nspectrum with some models developing storms in our far south late \nthis evening before quickly pushing into central IN/OH. Will \ntherefore hold off on another flood watch in our area, but \nHuntington, Wells and Adams Counties will be highly sensitive to any \nadditional rain and will watch those closely tonight.\n\nWe get a brief surge of high pressure/dry air advection tomorrow as \nthe front is shunted south. However, a more robust shortwave arrives \non Sat with another round of strengthening LLJ/theta-e advection. As \nexpected with this pattern there remains a high degree of \nuncertainty where and when the next round of rain falls but do \nexpect someone in the southern Great Lakes to get heavy rain during \nthis period given the degree of moisture being pumped into a quasi-\nstationary E-W boundary. Multiple rounds of convection are likely \nSat-Sun and may even extend into Mon. Additional flood watches for \nthis weekend are very possible. Pattern finally shifts a bit by the \nmiddle of next week with drier and cooler conditions expected.",
